# .env.example
# Template only, safe to commit. Copy and fill in real values:
#   install -m 600 .env.example .env
#
# WHY A TEMPLATE AND NOT AN ENCRYPTED FILE
# OWASP's guidance is a committed .env.example of placeholders plus a
# gitignored .env, which is the right tier here: one developer, one revocable
# Databricks Free Edition token, no production data. SOPS with age is the next
# step up and is what the FleetManagement repo uses -- worth graduating to when
# a second person needs these values, or when a credential guards anything that
# cannot simply be regenerated. Encrypted files buy nothing for a single holder
# of a throwaway token and cost real key-distribution overhead.
#
# WHY THESE ARE NOT IN conf/pipeline.yml
# PipelineConfig has no field a token could be pasted into. Credentials reach
# the process only through DatabricksSettings (pydantic-settings, env_prefix
# DATABRICKS_), so a credential cannot land in a tracked config file by
# accident rather than by policy.
#
# THE LEAK PATH THAT ACTUALLY BITES is not the file at rest -- it is the value
# in flight, inherited by every child process and swept into the first crash
# report. That half is handled by SecretStr, which keeps the token out of
# repr(), str(), logs and tracebacks, so a printed exception in a notebook
# cannot expose it.
#
# THIS REPO IS PUBLIC. Secret scanning and push protection are enabled, the
# pre-commit hook runs betterleaks over the index, and CI re-scans the whole
# history. Those are backstops. The control is that a real value never enters a
# tracked file: rotating after a leak does not un-leak the past, because copies
# survive in forks, mirrors and caches.

# Workspace URL, no trailing slash. Free Edition looks like:
#   https://dbc-XXXXXXXX-XXXX.cloud.databricks.com
DATABRICKS_HOST=

# Personal access token: Settings -> Developer -> Access tokens.
# Format is "dapi" followed by 32 hex characters.
DATABRICKS_TOKEN=
